{
 "version": 2,
 "rules": "149b39c8f55e9d41",
 "note": "The delayed-reward suite. Every position has a winning line for the player to move that an exhaustive search over that player's own decisions for the turn found, that the frozen heuristic does not find on any of the seeds agents are scored on, and that uniform random play does not stumble into on more than a quarter of them. 'max_turns' is the horizon, in the searched player's own turns: 1 means the line wins inside the searched turn, 2 means it cannot \u2014 it has to leave a board the frozen policy converts a turn later, with the rival answering in between, which is the delayed reward the training plan claims to teach. Only the searched turn is ever chosen, by the solver and by a scored agent alike; every later turn on both sides is the frozen policy. Positions are data with their verification attached; cptcg.learn.delayed.verify_suite re-derives all of it, and tests/learn/test_delayed_reward.py fails if a stored claim stops being true.",
 "positions": [
  {
   "id": "gear-before-the-raid",
   "kind": "board",
   "player": 0,
   "max_turns": 1,
   "source": "hand-built",
   "why": "Five Gigs and one ready 8-power Unit. Attacking now steals one Gig, which is the biggest immediate gain on the board and is what a one-ply agent takes; it ends the turn on six. Equipping the +2 Gear first is worth almost nothing on the board, but it lifts the attacker to 10 power, and steal_count(10) is two Gigs, which is the game. The plan's A-then-C in its smallest honest form \u2014 and the whole of it inside one turn, which is why the horizon is 1. Built like a real mid-game board \u2014 three Legends and a real remaining deck a side \u2014 so a value head reading list size, undrawn fraction and Legend RAM is not extrapolating on it.",
